<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        usesCleartextTraffic is spelled out because leaving it off does not mean "off": the platform
        default is permissive below API 28 and restrictive from 28 up, so an unset attribute is two
        different policies depending on the phone, and the permissive one covers exactly the API
        levels this app still supports at the bottom - 24 to 27.

        The app makes no requests of its own. The board, the dictionaries and every preference are
        local; the only traffic in the process belongs to play-services-ads and the UMP consent SDK.
        That is also the one thing to watch here, and the reason this is a decision rather than
        hygiene: an ad creative or a third-party impression tracker that still uses http is a
        creative that will not render, and a rewarded ad that does not render is coins the player was
        promised. The argument for taking that risk is that it is not a new one - from API 28 up the
        platform has been refusing cleartext for this app all along, which is nearly every device
        that will install it, and the ads work. This makes the remaining four API levels agree with
        the rest rather than being the quiet exception.

        If rewarded fill turns out to drop on API 24-27 specifically, this attribute is the first
        thing to put back, and a network security config scoped to the ad hosts is the second.

        The suppression below is for localeConfig, which lint reports as unused because it does
        nothing under API 33 - which is true, and is the whole design rather than an oversight. It
        is what puts Ravel in the system's per-app language picker on Android 13 and later, and the
        options tab is what covers the years below that; see res/xml/locales_config.xml for why the
        two settings do not fight. There is no way to scope tools:ignore to one attribute, so this
        covers the element: a later attribute here that is genuinely too new for minSdk will be
        silent, and the place to notice that is a review of this file rather than a warning.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".RavelApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Ravel"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        tools:ignore="UnusedAttribute">

        <!--
            The AdMob app this build belongs to. The ads SDK reads it here and crashes on the first
            request without it, so it is a manifest entry rather than anything the code can pass in.
            See res/values/ads.xml - the value there is currently a test identifier.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.ads.APPLICATION_ID"
            android:value="@string/admob_application_id" />

        <!--
            The ads SDK starts collecting app measurement data as soon as it is loaded, which is
            before the consent form has been answered. This holds that off until MobileAds.initialize
            is called - and that call is made only once the consent flow says ads may be requested,
            in AdConsent - so the first thing the SDK does is not something the player has yet been
            asked about.

            Worth knowing and invisible from here: play-services-ads merges in the
            com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID permission of its own accord. The app therefore
            has access to the advertising id and the Play Data safety form has to say so, whatever
            this app itself does with it, which is nothing directly.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.ads.DELAY_APP_MEASUREMENT_INIT"
            android:value="true" />

        <!--
            Portrait either way up, and no landscape.

            Every screen here is a column that must not scroll - a board is dragged across with a
            finger, and a grid that moves under that finger loses the drag - so the layout spends
            height it does not have on a phone lying on its side: the board is squeezed to a few
            pixels and the daily menu is cut off mid-card. This is a stopgap over that, not a design
            decision; a landscape layout would replace it.

            userPortrait rather than sensorPortrait, so a player who has turned auto-rotate off keeps
            it off. The half-turn is worth allowing on its own: a tablet or a phone on a charger is
            often upside down, and refusing it there is refusing the only orientation the device is
            in.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:screenOrientation="userPortrait"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            tools:ignore="LockedOrientationActivity">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
